l that being said uh we  need to make sure that the president's  successful when he's successful  everyone's successful  but to do that we have to make sure that  we deliver for the american people and  address their uh you know everyday  concerns right now  when it comes to something like the the  russia ukraine situation i mean i think  that  bad faith actors will criticize  uh the president and democrats no matter  what is done either as being weak or or  too uh  strong or not being friendly enough or  you know whatever the case may be bad  faith actors will have their criticisms  but  what would you like to see uh the the  the role of the biden administration in  the united states on the basis of what  happens there in the next few months  well actually i think that the  administration has done a tremendous job  of pulling together our allies onto the  same page with regard to  aggression on ukrainian  ukrainian borders  i think that  [Music]  what we need to see at this point is  more multilateral  coordination with regard to  what the costs russia would face were to  invade ukraine and that means agreeing  on a very  strong sanctions regime  agreeing on what we would do to support  ukraine if it is invaded  and of course  you know what do we do to give russia an  off-ramp uh with regard to the current  crisis  if we can do those three things  energetically and in a coordinated  fashion  i think that uh hopefully we'll get to a  better place but right now obviously is  a very dangerous moment  what what does your gut tell you and you  know you're on the house intelligence  committee but so so your gut and what  intelligence you've been presented there  may be some some gray area there but  what does your gut tell you about the  the real objective here of putin is this  to to suss out how people will will  react without a real plan to invade  it's very interesting and and um i had  the privilege of um  attending a classified briefing with  uh cia director bill burns who's a a  tremendous public servant by the way uh  we had this uh two-hour conversation on  monday  and without getting into classified  details i think that what i can say for  sure is that  uh putin has a very emotional connection  to  uh ukraine in the sense that it's almost  like a maniacal obsession with making  sure ukraine does not further  um kind of drift into the western orbit  interestingly all of his actions since  2014 the invasion of crimea  uh the  the presence of  russians and the don bass have further  uh pushed ukrainians into  the the embrace of western countries  the second observation i have is that um  i do think that  uh putin if he's just operating as a  rational actor and setting aside his  maniacal  kind of personal obsession with ukraine  knows that  all things being equal  he doesn't want to have a situation  where he invades and then has another  afghanistan on his hands just as the  soviet union did  30 years ago  and i think that  because of that  i do think that there will be a search  for  diplomatic options that allow him to  save face  even at the same time that he you know  kind of rattles the sabers  my hope is that his head prevails over  his heart  um and that we can kind of  uh  convince or persuade him that the cost  would just be prohibitive for him and  you know at the end of the day  he might  want to have on his tombstone that he  was the one who joined ukraine to russia  but the alternative could just as be  could be just as plausible which is he's  the one that lost ukraine  and so i think that's kind of the the  balance right now  is there uh  discussion amongst your colleagues about  the possibility that  in the the sort of using the ukraine  russia situation as cover that china may  see an opportunity to escalate  aggression with taiwan is that a topic  of consideration  it is um  i think there's  two reasons why potentially  she may not the the chinese premier xi  jinping may not move on taiwan right now  which is uh a i don't think that  uh china is prepared militarily to  invade taiwan and i my perception is  that  they don't think that they have  enough of a military advantage  over the united states in that region  that's my perception that's how i feel  and then the second is he himself is  trying to consolidate political power  this year  in china with the  i believe it's the chinese people's  congress that's happening in september  or october  and so he's also focused on making sure  that he can uh bring all the elites  along to basically um  rubber stamp  his uh  being in office  for longer than perhaps any other  premiere in many years  um a couple of other things and there's  really so much to talk about in terms of  inflation  um  similarly to gas prices where certainly  a president can do a little bit on some  of these issues but really these are big  structural systemic things that are  often global in nature  what can be done about inflation what  should be done about inflation  well i think it's a real issue and you  you don't have to go further than your  grocery store or your gas pump to see  that it's a real issue and anybody who  doesn't think so is denying reality  i think there are three things that  we as lawmakers and the buying  administration can do to tackle this  first is we have to fight covid  and we have to make sure that  we see  a decline in cases here but also abroad  so david in that regard i'm going to put  in a big plug for my legislation and my  moves to really get the world vaccinated  at this point not just the united states  because if we see variant after variant  come from abroad including the delta  variant which killed three of my distant  relatives in india and then the omicron  variant and others come to our shores  it's going to wreak more havoc work with  our economy and lead to more inflation  so that's the first thing we have to  control covid here and abroad the second  thing we have to do  is  you know those infrastructure  investments which we approved in the  fall  have to be directed at short-term  logistical bottlenecks  that  kind of continue to hamper the supply  chain so just as an example um we should  direct as much of that money as possible  in the short term to modernizing our  ports uh to making sure that our rail  facilities uh are modern and  capable of handling the surge in goods  that's happened now  um having pop-up inland ports and so  forth the third thing that we have to do  is we have to we have to um  call a spade a spade and  note that some companies are engaging in  predatory pricing practices  and on my committee the house oversight  subcommittee on economic and consumer  policy which is a mouthful we launched  an investigation into the meat  processing industry which  where four firms control more than 60  percent of meat processing and have  increased prices  double digits year after year and are  seeing net profits go through the roof  and we and that the same thing is  happening in other industries and so if  we do do those three things i think it  starts to help  but really what we need to do is make  sure that we can uh take the disruptions  out of the supply chain that will be the  biggest  help at this point  on uh to pick a couple of those things i  mean on vaccinating the world when you  look at a world map right now  we're basically talking about africa  where there's the serious lack of  vaccines i mean even if you know you  look at south america there's you know  in eastern europe there's still fewer  vaccine doses but but availability is  increasing rapidly  really everywhere but africa as far as i  understand looking at the map so is that  the area that should be the focus  i think that has to be uh among the top  places that we direct our efforts you  know in africa  it's still i think uh under 20  of the continent of 1.2 billion people  are vaccinated  and  i bet you you know dollars to donuts  that the next variant if it emerges is  going